        <p>The air was chilly with a hint of fall as Shane stepped out of his cab on the sunny September morning. He’d been to New York City before, but Flushing was a far cry from the bustle of Times Square. The pace was slower and the spirit calmer, similar to his native Ottawa. Queens suited Shane more than Manhattan, after all.</p>

<p>He approached the reception desk, giving the young man — an art student, if the Painters of the Renaissance textbook at his desk was any indication — his name and a friendly smile. The receptionist led him down the hall to the conference room, accolades from various Columbia University organizations lining the walls.</p>

<p>The receptionist rapped twice on the door to announce their presence, stepping back as it was opened by a tall person in a royal purple suit. A white pin announces their pronouns as they, them, and theirs.</p>

<p>“Hello, Shane Hollander, I presume. I’m Martine Thibodeaux. Welcome to Flushing! I’ll conduct your interview today.”</p>

<p>Their handshake is firm yet warm as Martine clasps Shane’s hand in both of theirs. Smooth, deep sepia skin covered their face, save for the emerging crow’s feet at their mahogany eyes as they smiled. It was difficult for Shane to guess, but he estimated Martine was in their early forties. Their tightly coiled black hair hung in chin-length twists around their face.</p>

<p>“Thanks, Martine! Flushing is refreshing; it actually reminds me of Ottawa. It’s hard to believe this is still New York City.”</p>

<p>“That’s why most of us like it here. Manhattan can be a lot. Flushing has more of a hometown feel, very communal. Many families have lived here for generations. Please, come in, take a seat. While we wait for Scott, we can chat.” Martine waves Shane over to the far end of the conference table. They take seats opposite each other, with a camera and laptop set up two chairs down from them.</p>

<p>Shane and Martine get into an impassioned discussion of the draft prospects — Shane argues that Vancouver native Lamont Silvers will go first overall, while Martine makes the case for Colorado’s Sequoyah Wilkes — when another knock draws their attention. The receptionist from earlier escorts Scott Hunter into the room; he’s the epitome of American cool in a black leather jacket, jeans, and an Admirals t-shirt. Shane felt overdressed in his black blazer and crisp button-down shirt.</p>

<p>“Scott, welcome! I’m Martine. Thank you for coming.”</p>

<p>“I’m glad to be here. Hey there, Rook!” Handshakes were exchanged, and Scott took his seat next to Shane.</p>

<p>Martine adjusted the camera one last time before reviewing the details.</p>

<p>“This is going to be livestreamed to viewers across North America, and the video will be on our website after we conclude. Please, relax, be honest, and have fun. We have a small but dedicated viewership, roughly two thousand followers who are excited to hear from two openly queer athletes. Do you have any questions for me?”</p>

<p>Scott claps his hands together, excitement clear in his face. “No, I’m good to go.”</p>

<p>“Same here. Let’s do this!” Shane found Scott’s energy infectious and started to relax as Martine picked up the remote to start the broadcast.</p>

<p>“Hello, welcome to OpenPlay, and thank you for tuning into our live broadcast today. I’m Martine Thibodeaux, reporter for OpenPlay Sports — Your Source for Queer Sports! I’m here today with two hockey legends, Shane Hollander of the Montreal Metros, and Scott Hunter of the New York Admirals. Gentlemen, thank you for being here today.”</p>

<p>“Thanks, Martine. It’s an honour to be here!” Shane replies with a genuine smile. He was just speaking to his fans, kids who were figuring out their identity, much like he was at their age.</p>

<p>“Glad to be invited, Martine!” Scott chimes in. Martine smiles before glancing briefly at the questions on their iPad.</p>

<p>“So let’s start with you, Shane. This past January, you came out in a very moving Instagram story. Can you tell us more about the story behind those photos?”</p>

<p><i>Oh, good, we’re starting with softball questions,</i> Shane thinks. His heart warms as he recalls his beloved grandparents, launching into their story.</p>

<p>“Well, my grandparents, Nakamura Masaki and Himari, emigrated to Canada from Japan in the early ‘70s. We’d watch the Metros every week, cheering them on together. They passed away before I was drafted, but I hope they’d be proud of how I carry their legacy. I write my mother’s name, ‘Yuna’, on my stick tape before every game I play.</p>

<p>“My family’s support has buoyed me through, and it’s brought us three Stanley Cups so far. That last pic is the most personal. I learned to skate on Rideau Lake, and I’ve always felt most <i>myself</i> on the ice. So what better place to celebrate my queerness than the banks of the Rideau?”</p>

<p>As tears well in his eyes, Shane looks up at Martine and sees his story had a similar effect on them. Even Scott is wiping the corners of his eyes after Shane finishes his response.</p>

<p>Martine keeps the emotional waves rolling with their next question directed at Scott. “And that legacy of courage runs deep in your family. Scott, your coming out story is a little different, but just as public. As viewers may remember, you kissed your boyfriend on centre ice after winning the Stanley Cup just months ago. Tell us what was going through your mind at that moment?”</p>

<p>“Well, my first thought was ‘Don’t trip, Scotty.’” Scott laughs, and Shane’s grateful he lightened the mood. “My second thought was, ‘Everybody has their loved ones on the ice, kissing their partners, I want that too.’ So I look up in the stands, and Kip is sitting with his friend Elena, clapping like the rest of the fans.</p>

<p>“I call him down before it’s even a fully formed thought. I just needed to celebrate with my man. He comes down, I escort him into the ice, and we both just go for it. Honestly, winning the Cup was nice, but getting to kiss Kip publicly was even better.”</p>

<p>Shane recalls watching that moment with his team, the pride he felt, and the excitement that one day he could do the same. “And I’d like to say, Scott, you opened a door I hadn’t even considered to be available. Because when I came out, I thought ‘Sure, I can date men and play hockey,’ but I didn’t think I could kiss a male partner on the ice. It, unfortunately, seemed to be a bridge too far, until you showed me the bridge was all in my head. So, thank you. Now I dream of kissing my partner on the ice one day.”</p>

<p>Fresh tears pool in Scott’s eyes at Shane’s comments, and he plucks a tissue from the box on the table. “That makes me very happy to hear, Rook. I’m glad the door is open wider for other queer players to run through.”</p>

<p>Martine nods and continues, “And we love to see it! My next question is for both of you: Unfortunately, homophobia is common on- and off-ice in the MLH. How do you manage the hatred thrown at you in these moments?”</p>

<p>Scott jumps in with his response. “If you don’t mind, I’d like to start this one off. Homophobia doesn’t fly in my locker room. You know I’m gay, but my Assistant Captain, Carter Vaughan, agrees that hate speech of any kind has no place in hockey. I’m very fortunate to have a solid group of guys on my team who are aligned on this. We call it out, shut it down, and report it when it becomes a pattern. That behaviour is absolutely unacceptable.”</p>

<p>Nodding his head, Shane adds, “I agree that homophobia, racism, and bigotry of any kind is unacceptable. It creates a hostile work environment. Although we play a sport for a living, it is our work, so the same rules apply as in any other office or workplace. As you said, Martine, homophobia has become an accepted part of the game’s culture. There’s chirping, which can be fun and playful and lighthearted, and then there’s bullying.</p>

<p>“When it comes to homophobia, that’s left chirping territory and entered bullying, harassment, hate speech territory. I have found that when I call out this behaviour as a queer person of colour, I’m told I’m being ‘too sensitive’ or ‘can’t take a joke’ because ‘everyone else is laughing’, which further marginalizes players who are outside the league’s  white, cisgender, heterosexual majority.”</p>

<p>Shane figures he hit on a point of personal interest as Martine dives deeper. “Why do you think that is? What can the league do to combat this systemic issue?”</p>

<p>“It’s a cultural issue, it’s a top-down issue more than a bottom-up issue,” Shane explains. “Because if the league made it clear in word and practice this behaviour was wrong, then it would stop in a heartbeat. If players got fined each time they dropped the f-slur, they’d stop using it. It’s not worth five grand just to call a player a slur. Right?</p>

<p>“It’s not worth sitting out a game just to call someone a slur. The problem is, players don’t feel the impact their words have. If they got slapped with a fine or suspended from games, they’d feel it, too. They’re hurting themselves then; losing money or ice time, seeing their stats drop, and letting someone else look like the star while they look like a loser.”</p>

<p>“Sounds like a fair trade to me,” Scott acquiesced. “You make an excellent point about the person saying the slur not knowing — or worse yet, not caring — about the effect their words have. I also think if players stopped encouraging the use of slurs and called them out more, we’d see the tide turn sooner.”</p>

<p>Martine leans forward in their seat, clearly excited about the passion these questions have elicited from Scott and Shane. “And what about from outside the arena? How do you handle homophobia elsewhere in your lives?”</p>

<p>Shane had just read some hateful comments on his Instagram post this morning. He had a lot to share in this regard. “I’ll start this one. As I’ve mentioned at the outset, I am Asian, and I get a lot of comments fetishizing my existence. They make assumptions about my sexuality that are demeaning to me personally. What I do sexually is nobody’s business except my partner and me. Beyond that, I generally block those senders and don’t engage with them because they want the fight. They want the spectacle, and I won’t give it to them. My hockey speaks loud enough.”</p>

<p>Scott looks simultaneously hurt on Shane’s behalf and proud of his restraint. “Yeah, I’ve seen some of those comments you get, Hollander, and you handle those bozos so well. What a lot of people don’t know is that Shane Hollander is probably the smartest guy I’ve ever met. You’re so witty, but it goes completely under the radar. He’s sent me some zippy one-liners in response to messages from bigots, and he clears them! I guarantee if you replied with that, then they’d shut up real quick!”</p>

<p>Martine scrolls on their iPad, glancing at the next question before continuing. “We have some questions from fans submitted on our blog and at OpenPlaySports across platforms. Our first question is for Shane from a fan by the name of Inshaneity24, and he asks, ‘Do you feel like your queerness and self-expression are sometimes misconstrued or distorted because of your identity as an Asian person?’”</p>

<p>Shane blushes, a mixture of pride at having inspired someone to build their online identity around him, and the weight of responsibility to live up to their esteem. “Inshaneity24, thanks for that, man! To answer your question, yes, the first thing people notice about me is that I’m Asian, so that’s the first filter they use to think of me. Then, there are the people who assume I’m ‘the girl’ in my relationships, and that’s both false thinking and deeply stereotypical. It’s the intersection of racism and misogyny to shrink me down into a stereotype that just doesn’t fit.</p>

<p>“I have a fluid, known skating style often compared to Yuzuru Hanyu—who is amazing, though I could never do his jumps. While commentators may mean well when they call me ‘twinkle toes’ or say I ‘flit past defensemen like a fairy in the woods,’ those descriptions are microaggressions that undermine my athleticism.</p>

<p>“I get comments from both players and hockey fans calling me ‘twink,’ ‘sissy,’ and a few other things that would probably be censored out of this. They see me as effeminate, soft, and weak. They want me to prove my masculinity by fighting back, but then call me a loose cannon when I do. I worry that the MLH might think twice before drafting the next queer Asian player, and I don’t want to create barriers for others to come after me.</p>

<p>“Conversely, when I ignore the comments and walk away from the situation, they just amp it up, say I’m not man enough to fight back. Eventually, they get tired and give up, but it’s annoying to say the least. I find that, in these cases, my masculinity takes a backseat or is entirely ignored because I’m gay and Asian. Loving men does not make me more feminine or less masculine. It makes me more me.”</p>

<p>Parched from his long response, Shane takes a generous sip from a glass of water placed on the table by an assistant before filming began. Sufficiently refreshed, Shane’s ready for Martine’s next question.</p>

<p>“Our next question is also for Shane and comes from a fan by the name of chaotichockeylesbian, who asks: ‘How does it feel to be forced into being a role model for many people who don't feel represented in the league?’” Martine is clearly in their element, proud of the community they’ve built to supply them with such astute questions for their guests today.</p>

<p>Scott also notices how popular Shane is as he receives a second direct query from a fan. “You’ve got quite a fanbase, Hollander!”</p>

<p>Shane, growing ever pinker, clears his throat as the emotions hit him once again. “I’m very lucky. Well, chaotichockeylesbian, thank you for asking this. It’s a double-edged sword, honestly. I feel very honoured and privileged to be someone people look up to. I’m one of a few Asians to ever play in the MLH, and the only openly gay Asian player in the league. I represent an ethnic and sexual minority in the MLH, a dream that, for many people of colour, queer people, and queer people of colour, could only be dreamed about.</p>

<p>“Unfortunately, there’s the other side of that — that this admiration and fandom I’ve garnered could all go away with one word or one false step. It’s immense pressure to be perfect all the time. I’m human and susceptible to making mistakes, and the world tends to be less forgiving when you represent marginalized populations.”</p>

<p>“Would you say the pressure comes more from within those populations or from outside of them?” Martine inquires, a question that Shane has learned to anticipate from his years of media training.</p>

<p>“It comes from both,” Shane clarifies. “Because some people erroneously believe that being a person of colour or queer automatically makes someone less capable, maintaining a standard of excellence, professionalism, politeness, and a flawless image serves to refute that. There’s also the pressure from your own community to represent them well. It feels great to make your people proud, but it’s devastating to disappoint them. It feels worse than letting yourself or your family down.”</p>

<p>With a look of recognition, having fought that same battle themselves, Martine nods sagely. They hold up their iPad with a video cued up: Leah in her Team Canada fleece, smiling excitedly. “We have a special video message from Team Canada’s own Leah Campbell.”</p>

<p>“Hi Shane and Scott! It’s a great pleasure to welcome you to the queer community.” Leah’s voice is cheerful, her expression bright with joy as she addresses them.</p>

<p>“Of course, the Women’s Hockey League has been supporting queerness in the league for the past two decades we’ve been in existence, but it’s nice to see the boys starting to catch up. Shane, we already know each other from Team Canada, and I couldn’t be prouder of you, dude. My question is, when do you think the MLH will finally open its arms to transgender players? Thanks, guys, and good luck next season!”</p>

<p>Shane feels lighter having seen a familiar face. “Always great to see Leah! And she makes a great point: the MLH is behind in welcoming queer players, particularly transgender players. I’d be honoured to have Max Riley on any line in the MLH. I welcome the challenge of playing against him because he’s one of the most formidable forwards I’ve ever seen. I’d also love to have him on the Metros, on my wing. Let’s get Max on the team for PyeongChang next year!”</p>

<p>Scott echoes Shane’s passion in his response. “Agreed, the MLH moves like a turtle when it comes to inclusion, but trans players deserve to play in professional hockey. Full stop. Anything less is discrimination. I’m with Hollander: put a dedicated player on my team or across from me playing their best hockey, and I’ll give them my best hockey, no matter how they identify.”</p>

<p>Martine swipes to the next video on their iPad, introducing it with a fond smile. “Our final question is a video sent in by a colleague you both know very well. Let’s watch.”</p>

<p>The screen fades in from black, revealing Ilya Rozanov in his Centaurs Pride jersey. “Hi Scott and Shane! You know I love chirping you both on the ice, but you are my favourite players in the league. Shane, we’ve been facing off against each other since we were in Juniors, and you always keep me guessing. I never know what to expect when we play, but I know it’ll be fun and exciting. You’re a real class act, setting an example in sportsmanship and character, personally and professionally.</p>

<p>“Scott, you may have invented hockey, but you keep changing the game. I have always found a safe space with you. We have this understanding that we keep challenging each other, we’re honest with each other, and that’s what makes you a great mentor.</p>

<p>“It’s the greatest honour of my life to say Shane Hollander and Scott Hunter are my friends. I’m so proud of you both for bravely telling the world your truth. I’ll be dancing at both of your weddings, so don’t forget my invitation! My question is actually for Scott: how does it feel to be a queer elder now? Thanks, Martine!”</p>

<p>“This guy.” Scott chuckles, shaking his head at Ilya’s antics. “I’m only three years older than him, let’s get that straight. But knowing other players will look at Hollander and me and say, ‘I can love who I love and play professional hockey’ is a very powerful thing. I am touched, truly.”</p>

<p>Shane’s trying to keep the tears from falling past his eyelashes. Ilya’s praise hit him harder than Ryan Price during his last game against Buffalo. “Yeah, I think it’s fair to say that Ilya and I have known each other our entire careers. There’s a mutual respect and a friendship that we’ve built over the years. He loves chirping, as he said, but it’s never malicious. He doesn’t use someone’s identity or weaknesses as material for chirping. People say Ilya’s an asshole, but he’s the kindest asshole you’ll ever meet.”</p>

<p>Martine is visibly pleased with the way the interview went. They look directly at Shane and Scott as they sign off. “Shane Hollander, Scott Hunter, thank you for your time today. That concludes our interview today. I’m Martine Thibodeaux, and this is OpenPlay Sports.”</p>

<p>Martine takes the remote from the table and stops the camera. “That was great! Thank you so much for sitting for this interview! I think you’ve made some excellent points and left our viewers with something to think about.”</p>

<p>“Thanks for the invitation, Martine,” Shane says, rising from his seat. “I look forward to seeing you in the press room soon.”</p>

<p>“Yeah, this was fun! So, same time next week?” Scott jokes, drawing a laugh from Martine.</p>

<p>“I’m sure our viewers still have questions. Maybe we’ll meet again soon.”</p>

<p>Scott and Shane exchange handshakes with Martine before leaving the conference room, following the short corridor out of the austere office space into a strip mall in Flushing, Queens.</p>

<p>“So what do you make of Rozanov’s video?” Scott asks as they step into the town car, off to meet Kip at Red Rooster.</p>

<p>“I know he said I always surprise him, but he surprises me more often than not. I gotta know, Scott, how does it feel to be a dad?”</p>

<p>“Hollander, you little shit!” Scott laughs despite his attempts to hold it in.</p>

<p>“Congrats, it’s a boy!” Shane cheers, head falling back against the seat as he loses himself in laughter.</p>

<p>“By the way, Rook, when’s the wedding? You and Rozanov make a handsome pair.”</p>

<p>“Joke's on you, we’re not even dating.”</p>

<p>“You do sound like an old married couple, though.”</p>

<p>Shane blushes, nervously tucking his hair behind his ear. He started letting it grow out, and the length took some getting used to as the shiny tresses brushed his ears. The thought of being married to Ilya was far from repulsive, and they clearly had chemistry together. So much so that even Scott Hunter noticed, but Shane resolved he wouldn’t pursue Ilya until he knew for sure his feelings were genuine.</p>

<p>Maybe one day he’d tell Scott what he and Ilya had been to each other, when Ilya was out and comfortable sharing that, but for now it remained a precious secret in Shane’s heart.</p>

<p>“If you say so, Scott.”</p>
